  "body": "What happens: a setup on work offset 5 or 6 posts through `assets/posts/sinumerik.cps` with the word `G58` / `G59` written verbatim in each section's opening block. The script's own notes say otherwise for this control: its comment near line 484 calls G58 / G59 \"axial programmable offsets\", `frameOf` (line 536) maps the settable frames as `G54..G57 -> 1..4, G505..G599 -> 5..99`, and its probing refusal says \"only the settable frames G54..G57 / G505..G599 can be set on this control\". So on an 840D the fifth and sixth settable frames are `G505` and `G506`; `G58` / `G59` do not select a work offset - the program would run in the wrong frame or alarm.\n\nFound while detailing #931 (2026-11-11). No golden covers offsets 5 or 6 on this post.\n\nTo do: confirm against Siemens' public programming documentation (settable zero offsets G54-G57, G505-G599; G58 / G59 axial programmable offset on the 840D sl), then either write `G505` / `G506` for offsets 5 and 6, or refuse those offsets by name on this post - record the choice in `docs/decisions/`. One source of truth: the work offset still comes only from the setup (`setup::wcs_slot`); only the word this post writes changes.\n\nRelated: #931 (work offsets as an index, the post supplies the word) would make this mapping the post's job by design; this issue is the wrong output today and does not wait for it.\n\nDone means: a pinning test posts setups on offsets 1-6 through the Sinumerik post and asserts the word for each (or the named refusal); the test lives with the post suites (#926 when it exists); no other post's goldens change.\n",
